steps.body:
Every class inherits `equals` from `Object`, and the default does exactly what `==` does: it is true only for the very same object. Two `new Point(1, 2)` calls build two objects, so both checks say `false`. Override `equals` to compare by value:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Point a = new Point(1, 2);
        Point b = new Point(1, 2);
        System.out.println("a == b: " + (a == b));
        System.out.println("a.equals(b): " + a.equals(b));
        System.out.println("a.equals(new Point(2, 1)): " + a.equals(new Point(2, 1)));
        System.out.println("a.equals(null): " + a.equals(null));
    }
}

class Point {
    private final int x;
    private final int y;

    Point(int x, int y) {
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
    }

    {{code}}
}
```

```java
@Override
public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) {
        return true;              // same object: nothing to compare
    }
    if (!(o instanceof Point)) {
        return false;             // also covers o == null
    }
    Point other = (Point) o;      // check first, then cast
    return x == other.x && y == other.y;
}
```

The parameter must be `Object`. Writing `equals(Point p)` only adds an overload, and `contains`, `indexOf`, and `HashSet` never call it because they call `equals(Object)`. With `@Override` on top, that mistake is a compile error instead of a silent bug.

Compare `int`, `char`, and `boolean` fields with `==` and object fields such as `String` with `equals`. When a field may be `null`, use `Objects.equals(a, b)` from `import java.util.Objects;`. It is true for two nulls and never throws:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        System.out.println(new Person("Ana", 30).equals(new Person("Ana", 30)));
        System.out.println(new Person("Ana", 30).equals(new Person("Ana", 31)));
        System.out.println(new Person(null, 30).equals(new Person(null, 30)));
        System.out.println(new Person(null, 30).equals(new Person("Ana", 30)));
    }
}

class Person {
    private final String name;
    private final int age;

    Person(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) {
            return true;
        }
        if (!(o instanceof Person)) {
            return false;
        }
        Person other = (Person) o;
        {{code}}
    }
}
```

```java
return age == other.age && Objects.equals(name, other.name);
```

