steps.body:
`HashSet` and `HashMap` find an element in two moves: `hashCode()` picks a bucket, then `equals` checks the few items in that bucket. Take a `Point` class whose `equals` compares its `x` and `y` fields. The inherited `hashCode` is based on identity, so two equal points almost always get different hash codes. The set compares hash codes before it calls `equals`, so it never sees the match and keeps both "duplicates".

The rule: **equal objects must have equal hash codes**. Unequal objects may share one, which only costs a little speed. So whenever you override `equals`, override `hashCode` from the same fields:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Point a = new Point(1, 2);
        Point b = new Point(1, 2);
        System.out.println("equal: " + a.equals(b));
        System.out.println("same hash: " + (a.hashCode() == b.hashCode()));
        Set<Point> seen = new HashSet<>(List.of(a, b));
        System.out.println("set size: " + seen.size());
    }
}

class Point {
    private final int x;
    private final int y;

    Point(int x, int y) {
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) {
            return true;
        }
        if (!(o instanceof Point)) {
            return false;
        }
        Point other = (Point) o;
        return x == other.x && y == other.y;
    }

    {{code}}
}
```

