        return x == other.x && y == other.y;
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(x, y);
    }
}

steps.body:
To give a class a natural order, implement `Comparable<X>` and its one method, `compareTo`. It returns a negative number when `this` comes first, zero on a tie, and a positive number when `this` comes after. Compare the most important field first and fall back to the next one on a tie:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        System.out.println("1.4 vs 2.0: " + new Version(1, 4).compareTo(new Version(2, 0)));
        System.out.println("1.10 vs 1.4: " + new Version(1, 10).compareTo(new Version(1, 4)));
        System.out.println("1.4 vs 1.4: " + new Version(1, 4).compareTo(new Version(1, 4)));
    }
}

{{code}}
```

```java
class Version implements Comparable<Version> {
    private final int major;
    private final int minor;

    Version(int major, int minor) {
        this.major = major;
        this.minor = minor;
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Version other) {
        int byMajor = Integer.compare(major, other.major);
        if (byMajor != 0) {
            return byMajor;
        }
        return Integer.compare(minor, other.minor);
    }
}
```

Use `Integer.compare`, never `major - other.major`, which can overflow.

`Collections.sort(list)`, `Collections.max`, `TreeSet`, and `TreeMap` all use `compareTo`. A `TreeSet` or `TreeMap` never calls `equals`: it treats `compareTo == 0` as a duplicate, so the set keeps only the first and the map reuses the key. If `compareTo` looked only at `major`, a `TreeSet` would keep just one 1.x version. So break ties on every field that `equals` uses: `compareTo` should return 0 exactly when `equals` is true, and the class overrides `equals` and `hashCode` on those same fields.

Natural order is one order. For any other, pass a `Comparator`:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        List<Version> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(new Version(1, 4), new Version(2, 0), new Version(1, 10)));
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(list);
    }
}

class Version implements Comparable<Version> {
    private final int major;
    private final int minor;

    Version(int major, int minor) {
        this.major = major;
        this.minor = minor;
    }

    public int getMinor() {
        return minor;
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Version other) {
        int byMajor = Integer.compare(major, other.major);
        if (byMajor != 0) {
            return byMajor;
        }
        return Integer.compare(minor, other.minor);
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return major + "." + minor;
    }
}
```

```java
list.sort(Collections.reverseOrder());               // newest first
list.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(Version::getMinor)); // by a getter
```

